(defmodule playground
  "The **Brood playground** (`M-x brood-playground`) — a scratch buffer that runs.

The tutorial's machinery pointed at nothing in particular: `*Playground*` is one ordinary,
fully editable Brood file where every top-level form evaluates as you settle typing, and each
one's result appears as ghost text at its end —

    (defn fac (n) (if (= n 0) 1 (* n (fac (dec n)))))
                                            => #<fn fac>
    (fac 5)                                 => 120  : int

— with the *Playground Spy* pane beside it showing what actually ran to produce the form your
cursor is in: every call and return, nested.

**Free text, no boxes.** Unlike the tutorial there is no generated prose to protect and
nothing is read-only; prose is `;` comments, because the buffer is Brood source and nothing
else. That is the whole difference between the two features, and it is why they share
`liveeval` (the notes vocabulary) and `workings` (the pane) rather than one being written in
terms of the other.

**Two things this needed from the language**, both added rather than worked around here:

  - `:trace :all` (`eval-server`) — trace every global the SESSION defined, resolved in the
    sandbox's own image. The tutorial had to guess a trace list by walking box source for
    call heads, which structurally cannot see a helper the source doesn't name. In a
    free-text buffer that indirection is the thing you opened the pane to watch.
  - `reflect/expr-type` — the checker's type for an anonymous expression. `type-signature`
    needs a global to ask about; what you just typed has no name.

**Evaluation is per top-level form, from the first changed one down.** The forms share one
sandbox session and definitions flow downward, so re-running only the form you touched would
leave a dependent form below showing a result from the old definition — a stale value that
looks current. Forms ABOVE the edit cannot depend on text below them and keep their results.

**The notes map is REBUILT every launch**, never patched. Patching is how a result outlives
the form it described: delete the last form and its `=> 6` stays behind, anchored to a line
that now holds something else. Rebuilding makes that unrepresentable — a note exists only if
this launch just put it there.

**Nothing persists.** A fresh buffer every time the editor starts: the point is a surface
with no stakes, and a playground that accumulates is a file you end up tidying. `C-x C-w` if
a session turns out to be worth keeping."
  (:use model) ; ed-buffer-index-by-name / message / ed-current-buf
  (:use commands) ; ed-show-named / ed-select
  (:use interactive) ; defcommand — M-x brood-playground
  (:use editor/buffer) ; buffer-text / buffer-rope / annotate
  (:use editor/layers) ; register-type-layers / request-close
  (:use theme) ; *subtext* / *teal* — the spy pane's two faces
  (:use sandbox)) ; sandbox-start / sandbox-eval / the session
;; liveeval, workings, sandbox-events, results, sexp and modes are referenced qualified
;; below — each `mod/name` auto-loads its module.

(def *buffer-name* "*Playground*")
(def *spy-buffer-name* "*Playground Spy*")
(def *playground-timeout-ms* 5000)

;; Where this pane's state lives and what it is called — see `workings`, which owns the
;; behaviour. `:key` keeps it clear of the tutorial's pane in the same model.
(def *spy-spec*
  {:key :playground
   :buffer playground/*spy-buffer-name*
   :type :playground-spy
   :noun "form"
   :heading "What ran"})

(def *welcome*
  ;; Deliberately tiny. A playground that opens full of somebody else's examples is a file
  ;; you clear before you can use it; two lines say what the surface does and get out of
  ;; the way. It is ordinary editable text like everything else here — delete it.
  (str ";; Brood playground — every top-level form runs as you stop typing.\n"
    ";; Results appear at the end of each form; the pane on the right shows what ran.\n\n"
    "(+ 1 2)\n"))

;; ---- the buffer ----------------------------------------------------------------

(defn playground-buffer? (b)
  "Is buffer `b` the playground? Keyed on the buffer NAME, like every other report buffer
here. Its TYPE is `:playground`, which stacks a two-hook layer over the real brood-mode —
so it is a Brood file first and a playground second."
  (and b (= (:name b) *buffer-name*)))

(defn playground-live-buffer? (m)
  "Is *Playground* on screen — i.e. does the playground still want a sandbox? One of the
`sandbox-events` client hooks; it is what stops a dead child being let go while this is open."
  (not (nil? (ed-buffer-index-by-name m *buffer-name*))))

(defn- playground-live?
  ()
  ;; Is there a running editor to deliver a sandbox event to? A PROCESS-global fact, not a
  ;; model one: without the `ui-run` loop a fire-and-forget request has nowhere to come back
  ;; to, so the launcher evaluates in-image instead of stranding every form on `evaluating…`.
  ;; It also gates spawning the sandbox at all — a headless open (a test, a `--headless`
  ;; daemon) has no use for a child runtime and should not start one.
  (not (nil? (proc/whereis :editor))))

(defn- pg-index (m) (ed-buffer-index-by-name m *buffer-name*))

(defn- pg-buf
  (m)
  (let (i (pg-index m)) (when i (nth (:buffers m) i))))

(defn- pg-update-buf
  (m f)
  ;; Apply `f` to the playground buffer wherever it sits in the pool, leaving the rest of the
  ;; model alone. Every note-stamping path goes through here so none of them has to care
  ;; whether the playground is the selected buffer (it usually isn't when a reply lands).
  (let (i (pg-index m))
    (if (nil? i) m (assoc m :buffers (assoc (:buffers m) i (f (nth (:buffers m) i)))))))

;; ---- the forms, and which of them changed ---------------------------------------
;;
;; Every entry point parses ONCE and threads the result. `sexp/top-level-forms` builds a CST
;; of the whole buffer, and the two hot paths here (a launch over N forms, and `:post-key` on
;; every keystroke) each used to call it per form — N+1 full parses for one keystroke.

(defn forms-of (text)
  "The buffer's top-level forms as positioned nodes. Error-tolerant, so it answers over text
someone is mid-typing — whether a given form *evaluates* is `liveeval/parse-state`'s
separate question."
  (sexp/top-level-forms text))

(defn form-srcs-in (text forms)
  "`{index -> source}` for already-parsed `forms` — the shape a launch compares against last
time's to find what changed."
  (fold (fn (acc iv)
          (let ([i node] iv)
            (assoc acc i (string/substring text (:start node) (:end node)))))
    {}
    (seq/enumerate forms)))

(defn form-srcs (text)
  "`{index -> source}` for `text`, parsing it. The convenience form of [[form-srcs-in]]."
  (form-srcs-in text (forms-of text)))

(defn form-index-at (text forms point)
  "Which top-level form `point` falls in, or nil. Takes already-parsed `forms` — it is called
from `:post-key`, so it may not parse."
  (first (filter (fn (i)
                   (let (node (nth forms i nil))
                     (and node (>= point (:start node)) (<= point (:end node)))))
           (range (count forms)))))

(defn first-changed (srcs old-srcs total)
  "The index of the first form whose source differs from last time, or nil when none does.

Everything from here down is re-sent; everything above keeps its result. That is the
dependency direction — a form cannot depend on text below it — and it is what stops a
one-character edit re-running the whole buffer, which for a playground full of loops and
spawns is the difference between a live surface and a stuttering one."
  (let (n (math/max total (count old-srcs)))
    (first (filter (fn (i) (not= (get srcs i) (get old-srcs i))) (range n)))))

;; ---- notes: a result at the end of its form --------------------------------------

(defn- pg-set-note
  (m i note forms text)
  ;; Stamp `note` for form `i` through the shared anchored-note channel: its answer on the
  ;; line below the form's last, its `:hint` (the derived type `liveeval/result-note` put
  ;; there) at the form's opening line. `results/set-note` owns everything else — the
  ;; owner-keying, the hint key, and the one-line collapse when a form at EOF has no line
  ;; below it. `forms`/`text` are passed in because the callers already have them.
  (let (node (nth forms i nil))
    (if (nil? node)
      m
      (pg-update-buf m
        (fn (bb)
          (results/set-note bb
            i
            note
            (results/answer-line text (dec (:end node))) ; `:end` is exclusive
            (results/line-of text (:start node))))))))

(defn- pg-clear-notes
  (bb)
  ;; Drop every note. A launch rebuilds the map from nothing, so a note for a form that has
  ;; been deleted cannot survive — the alternative (patching in place) leaves the result of a
  ;; form that no longer exists sitting on whatever line took its place.
  (annotate bb :eol-notes {}))

;; ---- eval-on-type: the stale? / launch / apply triple ------------------------------

(defn ed-playground-stale? (m)
  "O(1): the selected buffer is the playground and its rope moved since the last launch —
arms the `:playground-eval` idle timer (`model/ed-sync-timers`)."
  (let (b (ed-current-buf m))
    (and (playground-buffer? b) (not= (buffer-rope b) (:playground-rope b)))))

(defn ed-playground-launch (m)
  "The debounce landed: re-evaluate every top-level form from the first changed one down.

A form that is mid-typing (reader E0002) says so in the pending voice and keeps nothing
stale; one that is genuinely broken fails locally with no round trip; a blank one says
nothing. Each sent form carries `:trace :all`, so the pane can show what it actually called
rather than what its own text happened to name.

Notes are rebuilt, not patched — see the module docstring. A form whose reply is still in
flight wears `⋯ evaluating…` rather than nothing, so a rebuild never flashes the buffer
blank on its way to the same answer."
  (let (b (pg-buf m))
    (if (nil? b)
      m
      (let (text (buffer-text b)
            rope (buffer-rope b)
            forms (forms-of text)
            srcs (form-srcs-in text forms)
            old-srcs (or (:playground-srcs b) {})
            old-notes (or (results/eol-notes-any b) {})
            live (playground-live?)
            from (first-changed srcs old-srcs (count forms))
            start-id (get-in m [:playground :next-id] 1)
            ;; Drop the cached cascades of everything being re-sent (they describe code that
            ;; no longer exists) and clear the notes, before anything runs.
            m0 (pg-update-buf (workings/forget *spy-spec* m from)
                 (fn (bb)
                   (pg-clear-notes (assoc bb :playground-rope rope :playground-srcs srcs))))
            note-at (fn (mm i note) (pg-set-note mm i note forms text))
            result (if (nil? from)
                     ;; nothing changed (whitespace the reader ignores, an edit undone):
                     ;; re-anchor every note where it now sits and send nothing. Form keys
                     ;; only: a hint (`results/hint-key`) is derived from its result note, so
                     ;; re-stamping the results re-anchors the hints too — while re-stamping
                     ;; a hint directly would index `forms` with its `[:hint i]` key.
                     [(fold (fn (mm kv)
                              (let ([i note] kv) (note-at mm i (dissoc note :line))))
                        m0
                        (filter (fn (kv) (int? (first kv))) old-notes))
                       start-id
                       {}]
                     (fold
                       (fn (acc i)
                         (let ([m1 next-id pending] acc
                               src (get srcs i "")
                               old (get old-notes i)
                               state (if (< i from) :carry (liveeval/parse-state src)))
                           (match state
                             ;; above the edit: unaffected, so keep the verdict as it is
                             (:carry (if old
                                       [(note-at m1 i (dissoc old :line)) next-id pending]
                                       acc))
                             (:blank acc)
                             ;; Mid-form. Neither re-stamp the old verdict (a confident
                             ;; `=> 6` beside a form that can no longer produce it) nor show
                             ;; nothing (indistinguishable from "no feedback yet"): say what
                             ;; it is waiting for, dim.
                             ([:incomplete reason] [(note-at m1
                                                      i
                                                      {:kind :pending :text (str "… " reason)})
                                                     next-id pending])
                             ([:err msg] [(note-at m1
                                            i
                                            {:kind :fail :error? true :text (str "✗ " msg)})
                                           next-id pending])
                             ;; Live (an `:editor` process exists): fire the request at the
                             ;; sandbox and let the reply land through `sandbox-events`.
                             ;; Headless (tests, no loop to deliver an event to): evaluate
                             ;; in-image, synchronously — the diag-fallback discipline the
                             ;; tutorial follows. It yields values but no cascade: `:trace
                             ;; :all` resolves against the SANDBOX's session, and the
                             ;; editor's own image is not it.
                             (:ok (if live
                                    (do
                                      (sandbox-eval next-id src *playground-timeout-ms* :all)
                                      [(note-at m1 i {:kind :pending :text "⋯ evaluating…"})
                                        (inc next-id)
                                        (assoc pending next-id {:region i :rope rope})])
                                    [(note-at m1
                                       i
                                       (liveeval/result-note (eval-server/eval-capturing src nil)
                                         src))
                                      next-id pending])))))
                       [m0 start-id {}]
                       (range (count forms))))
            [m2 next-id pending] result)
        (update m2
          :playground merge
          {:next-id next-id :pending (merge (get-in m2 [:playground :pending] {}) pending)})))))

;; ---- the sandbox client hooks (registered in `sandbox-events/*clients*`) ------------

(defn playground-sandbox-ready (m gen)
  "The child announced itself (first boot, or a reset): remember its generation and clear the
eval stash so the next idle beat replays the whole buffer against the fresh, empty session.
Clearing `:playground-srcs` is the load-bearing half — leaving it behind would make the
restart re-send nothing, and every form would look evaluated against a session that no longer
holds any of its definitions."
  (pg-update-buf (update m :playground merge {:sandbox-gen gen :sandbox-ready true :pending {}})
    (fn (bb) (assoc bb :playground-rope nil :playground-srcs {}))))

(defn playground-sandbox-reply (m reply gen)
  "Fold one sandbox reply into the model. A reply the playground never sent, one from a
superseded child, or one whose buffer has moved on since the send is dropped — the next idle
beat re-evaluates. Otherwise the result lands as the form's note and its trace as the pane's
cascade."
  (let (id (get reply :id)
        pend (get-in m [:playground :pending id]))
    (cond
      (nil? pend) m ; not ours (the tutorial's, or a straggler) — the fan-out's no-op
      (not= gen (get-in m [:playground :sandbox-gen])) m
      else
        (let (m1 (update m :playground update :pending dissoc id)
              b (pg-buf m1)
              i (:region pend))
          (if (or (nil? b) (not= (buffer-rope b) (:rope pend)))
            m1
            (let (text (buffer-text b)
                  forms (forms-of text)
                  src (get (form-srcs-in text forms) i)
                  m2 (pg-set-note m1 i (liveeval/result-note reply src) forms text))
              ;; `workings/show` caches this form's cascade and opens the pane only if the
              ;; cascade is real AND belongs to the form the cursor is in — so a background
              ;; re-eval never yanks the pane off what you are reading.
              (workings/show *spy-spec* m2 i (form-index-at text forms (:point b 0)) reply)))))))

(defn playground-sandbox-down (m code)
  "The child went away and somebody still wants a sandbox (letting it go for good is
`sandbox-events`' call — only it can see whether every client is gone)."
  (let (m1 (update m :playground merge {:sandbox-ready false}))
    (cond
      (not (playground-live-buffer? m1)) m1 ; not ours to narrate
      (= code :gave-up) (message m1 "playground sandbox keeps dying — *Messages* may say why")
      (= code :spawn-failed)
        (message m1 "could not start the playground sandbox (is `nest` on PATH?)")
      else (message m1 "playground sandbox restarting…"))))

;; ---- the pane follows the cursor -----------------------------------------------------

(defn spy-on-screen? (m)
  "Is the *Playground Spy* pane currently on screen?"
  (not (nil? (ed-buffer-index-by-name m *spy-buffer-name*))))

(defn playground-post-key (m-before m key)
  "The playground's `:post-key` hook: keep the spy pane following the cursor from form to
form, with no re-evaluation. One parse, and it only touches the buffer when the form under
point actually changed — this runs on every keystroke."
  (let (b (ed-current-buf m))
    (if (not (playground-buffer? b))
      m
      (let (text (buffer-text b)
            i (form-index-at text (forms-of text) (:point b 0)))
        (if (nil? i) m (workings/follow *spy-spec* m i))))))

;; ---- the modes: a Brood buffer that runs, and a read-only pane beside it -------------

;; The playground layer adds exactly two things to brood-mode: the `:post-key` hook that
;; keeps the pane following the cursor, and an `:on-close` that takes the pane with it. It
;; STACKS over the real brood-mode rather than replacing it, so the buffer keeps every
;; language service a `.blsp` file has — highlighting, indent, eldoc, completion, the
;; structural motions. A playground where `(` didn't indent would be a worse place to try
;; the language than the file you already had open.
(def playground-mode-layer
  {:name 'playground
   :post-key 'playground/playground-post-key
   :hooks {:on-close '(playground/playground-on-close)}})

(register-type-layers :playground
  (list 'playground/playground-mode-layer
    'modes/brood-mode-layer 'modes/prog-mode-layer 'modes/text-mode-layer))

(defn- spy-line-face
  (line)
  (cond
    (string/starts-with? line "What ran") {:fg *subtext*}
    (string/starts-with? (string/triml line) "= ") {:fg *teal*}
    else nil))

(defn spy-fontify (src)
  "The spy pane's colouring: the heading dim, every `= value` return in the accent — so a
cascade reads as calls going in and values coming back rather than as a wall of text."
  (modes/fontify-line-faces src playground/spy-line-face))

(def playground-spy-layer
  {:name 'playground-spy :no-line-numbers true :fontify 'playground/spy-fontify})

(register-type-layers :playground-spy
  (list 'playground/playground-spy-layer 'modes/special-mode-layer 'modes/text-mode-layer))

(defn playground-on-close (buf)
  "The `:on-close` hook: killing *Playground* takes its pane with it — the pane is a view of
the playground's forms and says nothing without them, so leaving it behind leaves a corpse on
screen. `request-close` NAMES the pane rather than closing it: a hook is handed only its own
buffer, and it is the app that knows the buffer pool.

The sandbox is deliberately NOT stopped here. The tutorial may still be using it, and
`sandbox-events` is the only place that can see whether every client is gone."
  (editor/layers/request-close buf *spy-buffer-name*))

;; ---- the commands ---------------------------------------------------------------------

(defcommand cmd-brood-playground
  (m key)
  "Open the Brood playground (M-x brood-playground, C-c p p): a free-text `*Playground*`
buffer whose top-level forms evaluate as you settle typing, results as ghost text, and a
*Playground Spy* pane beside it showing what each form actually called.

**One command, not two.** Running it again switches to the buffer you already have — and
brings the pane back if you closed it, on the form the cursor is in. A separate
`playground-spy` command was a second thing to know for what is really the same intent
(\"show me the playground\"), and a second entry cluttering `M-x` for a reader who has no
idea the pane can be dismissed. Closing the pane still latches, so a later result does not
reopen it behind you; asking for the playground again does, because that is you asking."
  (do
    ;; The sandbox is a child runtime, and only a running editor can receive its replies —
    ;; so a headless open (a test, a `--headless` daemon) starts nothing.
    (when (playground-live?)
      (sandbox-events/ensure) ; the UiEvent impls live there; loading it IS registering them
      (sandbox-start))
    (let (existing (pg-index m))
      (if existing
        ;; already open: select it, and un-latch the pane onto the form at point
        (let (m1 (ed-select m existing)
              b (pg-buf m1)
              text (when b (buffer-text b)))
          (if (nil? b)
            m1
            (workings/reopen *spy-spec* m1 (form-index-at text (forms-of text) (:point b 0)))))
        ;; `:playground` stacks a two-hook layer over the real brood-mode, so the buffer is a
        ;; Brood file first — highlighting, indent, eldoc and paredit all come for free.
        ;;
        ;; The pane opens HERE, with `force`, rather than waiting to earn its split from the
        ;; first traced reply the way the tutorial's does. The rules differ because the
        ;; features do: a lesson is prose you are reading and the pane is an aside; a
        ;; playground IS the pane's subject — you opened it to watch code run.
        (message (workings/refresh *spy-spec*
                   (ed-show-named m *buffer-name* *welcome* :playground)
                   0
                   true)
          "playground — type Brood; it runs as you stop typing")))))
